## 3. Directory map

```
app.py                 KEEP — legacy routes + include_router(v2) mounts
db.py                  KEEP — legacy `runs` table access
correct_pptx.py        KEEP
rules.yaml             KEEP — legacy mechanical rule set (distinct from the new Rule Library)
static/index.html      KEEP — legacy UI

validator/             KEEP — mechanical brand checks, unchanged
  checks.py  core.py  llm_check.py  models.py  report_pdf.py  report_pptx.py  rules.py

frontend/              NEW — React/Vite: upload, progress, results, history, admin
agents/                NEW — ReviewAgent ABC + Visual/Content/CIOLens/Critic/ProposalContext
                             (+ registry entries for future agents)
llm/                   NEW — LLMProvider ABC, provider factory, per-provider implementations
  provider.py              — LLMProvider ABC + ProviderFactory (reads LLM_PROVIDER from env)
  providers/
    gateway_provider.py    — InfoBeans gateway (gateway.creatingwow.in)
    litellm_provider.py    — LiteLLM wrapper (routes to Claude, Groq, Gemini, OpenAI, etc.)
    claude_provider.py     — Anthropic Claude direct
    groq_provider.py       — Groq direct
    gemini_provider.py     — Google Gemini direct
    openai_provider.py     — OpenAI direct
    mock_provider.py       — deterministic test doubles, never calls any real API
renderer/              NEW — SlideRenderer ABC, LibreOfficeRenderer, UnavailableRenderer, factory
prompts/               NEW — seed YAML files loaded into DB on first run, one folder per agent
                             (source of truth for initial versions; DB is authoritative at runtime)
rule_library/          NEW — versioned rule YAML/JSON, one folder per domain
                             (branding, typography, template, security, accessibility)
services/              NEW — ReviewService (orchestrator), PPTService, PromptService,
                             RuleService, ObservabilityService, FixService,
                             SlideDesignMatchService
api/                   NEW — FastAPI v2 routers: review, prompts, rules, agents,
                             observability, health
db/                    NEW — migrations + v2 repository + models
slide_templates/       NEW — reference PPT slides used for design-match exclusion
```

---

## 4. Orchestration model — Agent DAG

The orchestrator is deterministic. It builds an execution plan from the Agent Registry and a Review
Profile (which agents are enabled for this review), then executes it as a DAG:

```
Review Request
│
├── PPTX (required)
├── Review Profile (required)
├── Reference Template PPT (optional) ← Slide Design Match
└── Proposal Context (optional)       ← Proposal Agent

                    Extraction (always first)
                         │
         ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
         │               │                │
    Template         Branding         Security
    Accessibility
         │
         ▼
    [Slide Design Match Filter]  ← if reference PPT provided,
         │                          matched slides are excluded
         │                          from all downstream agents
         ▼
    Content Extraction
         │
    ┌────┼───────────────┬─────────────────┐
    │    │               │                 │
 Content Story      Customer          Proposal
 Agent  Agent        Agent             Context
                                        Agent
                          │                 │
                          └────────┬────────┘
                                   ▼
                        Cross-Slide / CIO Lens
                                   │
                                   ▼
                               Critic
                                   │
                                   ▼
                    Aggregator → score + findings + severity
                                   │
                    ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
                    ▼                             ▼
             Human Review                    Fix Agent
          (HUMAN_APPROVAL /               (AUTO_FIX only)
           RECOMMENDATION_ONLY)                  │
                                                 ▼
                                           New PPT → Re-review
                                   │
                                   ▼
              Customer Quality Gate: 🟢 READY / 🟡 REVIEW / 🔴 BLOCKED
```

**Rules for adding a new agent to the DAG:**

- Register it in the Agent Registry with `agent_id`, `type` (`rule | llm | hybrid`),
  `execution_mode` (`parallel | sequential`), and its dependency (what extraction stage it needs).
- Give it a Review Profile toggle so it can be enabled/disabled per customer/project without a code change.
- Do not add ad-hoc `if agent_name == "..."` branching in `ReviewService` — the orchestrator reads the
  registry, it doesn't special-case agents.

---

## 5. Standard Agent Contract

Every agent, regardless of type, returns exactly this shape (Python: a dataclass in `agents/base.py`;
wire format: JSON):

```json
{
  "agent_id": "customer_focus",
  "status": "completed",
  "score": 78,
  "findings": [
    {
      "slide": 8,
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "category": "customer_relevance",
      "title": "Too technical",
      "description": "The slide focuses heavily on implementation details.",
      "recommendation": "Emphasize customer outcomes instead of internals.",
      "confidence": 0.91,
      "action_type": "RECOMMENDATION_ONLY",
      "rule_id": null,
      "prompt_version_id": 5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "..."
}
```

When writing an LLM-based agent, force structured output against this schema (or a subset of it) at the
prompt level, and validate the parsed response before constructing the dataclass. Never pass raw model
text further down the pipeline.

---

## 6. Rule Library vs. Prompt Registry — keep them separate

|                  | Rule Library                                              | Prompt Registry                                        |
|------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| Used by          | Rule-type and hybrid agents                               | LLM-type agents                                        |
| Format           | Versioned YAML/JSON per domain (`rule_library/branding/v2.yaml`) | Versioned YAML per agent (`prompts/customer_focus/v5.yaml`) |
| Storage          | Loaded into DB tables `rules` / `rule_versions` on startup | Loaded into DB tables `prompt` / `prompt_version` on startup |
| Mutability       | Versions immutable once loaded; new changes = new version | Same — immutable once created                          |
| Activation       | A rule set version is activated per business unit/project | A prompt version is activated globally or per Review Profile |
| Applies-to scope | e.g. "Minimum Body Font: 16pt, applies to Customer + Sales, not Internal" | e.g. a prompt can be scoped to a Review Profile via `prompt_overrides` |

Never let an agent read a rule or a prompt string from anywhere except these two registries. If you're
tempted to inline a threshold or a system prompt in agent code "just for now," stop — add a versioned
entry instead.

---

## 7. Review Profiles — configuration, not code

A Review Profile is what makes "the same PPT gets a different review for a CTO audience vs. a CEO
audience" possible without branching code:

```json
{
  "review_profile": "customer_presentation",
  "customer": "ABC Corporation",
  "audience": "CTO + Business Leadership",
  "objective": "Get approval for proposed solution",
  "industry": "Banking",
  "presentation_level": "executive",
  "agents": [
    { "id": "template",          "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "branding",          "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "customer_focus",    "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "storytelling",      "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "proposal_context",  "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "architecture",      "enabled": false }
  ]
}
```

The API and frontend must support both **Run All** (build the full plan from the profile) and
**Run Selected** (execute a subset the orchestrator resolves against dependencies). Never hardcode a
profile in Python — profiles are data, stored and versioned like everything else.

---

## 8. Fix Agent discipline

- The Fix Agent only ever touches findings marked `AUTO_FIX` (e.g., "body font 12px, rule requires
  16px minimum" → mechanically correct).
- `HUMAN_APPROVAL` and `RECOMMENDATION_ONLY` findings are surfaced to a human; the Fix Agent must never
  auto-apply them, even if a prompt or a user asks it to "just fix everything."
- Every fix is logged in `fix_operations` (what changed, which finding triggered it, before/after) and
  triggers a re-review, not a re-score by assumption — always re-run the affected agents.

---

## 9. Testing expectations

- Every new agent needs a `MockProvider`-backed unit test — no test may call the live gateway.
- Renderer tests must cover both `LibreOfficeRenderer` (if `soffice` present in CI) and
  `UnavailableRenderer` (always runs).
- Orchestrator/DAG tests should assert dependency ordering, not just final output — a Content Agent that
  accidentally runs before extraction is a regression even if the final score looks right.
- Golden-dataset evaluation (Section 16 of the architecture notes) is a separate, longer-running suite —
  do not fold it into the standard unit test run.
- Slide Design Match tests must cover: exact match, partial match, no match, and missing reference PPT
  (graceful degradation — never fail the pipeline).

---

## 10. Commands

```bash
# Backend
uvicorn app:app --reload --port 8000

# Frontend
cd frontend && npm run dev
cd frontend && npm run build   # produces frontend/dist, served at /app

# Tests
pytest tests/

# DB migration (idempotent, additive)
python db/migrate.py
```

---

## 11. When extending this system

Checklist for "I need to add a new reviewer, e.g. a Legal Compliance Agent":

1. Add an entry to the Agent Registry (`agent_id`, `type`, `version`, `severity default`,
   `execution_mode`, `dependency stage`).
2. If rule-based: add a versioned rule set under `rule_library/legal/`. If LLM-based: add a versioned
   prompt under `prompts/legal_compliance/v1.yaml` with an explicit output schema.
3. Wire the dependency into the DAG (which extraction stage does it need?).
4. Add it to relevant Review Profiles as `enabled: true/false`.
5. Write a `MockProvider`-backed test.
6. Do not touch any file listed as legacy in Section 3.
7. Update the Prompt/Rule admin UI listing if it's user-facing (it's data-driven, so this is usually
   automatic — verify, don't assume).

If a task would require breaking rules 1–9 above to complete, stop and flag it rather than proceeding.

---

## 12. Slide Design Match — reference template exclusion

### What it is

The user may optionally supply a **Reference Template PPT** alongside the deck under review. This PPT
contains one or more slides whose visual design (layout, background, colour zones, placeholder positions)
represents the approved template. Any slide in the review deck whose design **matches** a reference slide
is excluded from design-related review agents — it has already passed the design bar.

This is not a content bypass. Only design-layer findings are suppressed for matched slides. Content,
story, proposal, and security agents still run on every slide.

### Why it exists

When a team builds a deck on the approved master template, flagging every title slide or section divider
for "wrong layout" is noise. The match filter removes that noise without disabling the template agent
globally.

### How matching works

Matching is **structural and visual, not pixel-perfect**:

| Signal                        | Weight |
|-------------------------------|--------|
| Slide layout name             | High   |
| Background fill (colour/image hash) | High |
| Placeholder count + types     | Medium |
| Placeholder positions (normalised) | Medium |
| Font family on master         | Low    |

A slide is considered **matched** when the weighted similarity score exceeds the configured threshold
(default `0.85`, configurable per Review Profile).

Matching is performed by `SlideDesignMatchService` before content extraction. The service returns a
`matched_slide_indices` list that the orchestrator attaches to the review context. Agents that respect
the exclusion check this list before emitting design findings.

### Agents that honour the exclusion

| Agent            | Respects match exclusion? | Reason                                      |
|------------------|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| Template Agent   | Yes                      | Design compliance — excluded if matched     |
| Branding Agent   | Yes                      | Visual brand — excluded if matched          |
| Accessibility    | Partial                  | Colour contrast still checked; layout skipped |
| Content Agent    | No                       | Content is independent of design            |
| Story Agent      | No                       | Narrative is independent of design          |
| Customer Agent   | No                       | Messaging is independent of design          |
| Proposal Agent   | No                       | Requirement coverage is independent of design |
| Security Agent   | No                       | Security is independent of design           |
| CIO Lens         | No                       | Deck-level; operates on all slides          |

### Input

```
Review Request
├── PPTX                    (required)
├── Review Profile          (required)
├── Reference Template PPT  (optional) ← new optional input
└── Proposal Context        (optional)
```

The reference PPT is uploaded as a separate file. If absent, the match filter is skipped entirely —
no error, no degraded score, no pipeline change.

### Reference PPT storage and versioning

- Stored as a versioned artifact: `reference_template_id` + `reference_template_version`.
- Included in the audit trail so "why was slide 3 not reviewed?" is always answerable.
- A team can register a shared reference PPT once and reuse it across multiple review runs.

### Review Profile configuration

```json
{
  "review_profile": "customer_proposal",
  "slide_design_match": {
    "enabled": true,
    "reference_template_id": "infobeans-master-v3",
    "match_threshold": 0.85,
    "exclusion_scope": ["template", "branding"]
  }
}
```

To disable for a profile:

```json
"slide_design_match": { "enabled": false }
```

### Agent Registry — SlideDesignMatchService

```yaml
service_id: slide_design_match
type: rule
version: 1
execution_mode: sequential
dependency_stage: extraction
requires:
  - pptx
  - reference_template_ppt
enabled_by_default: false
```

### Failure behaviour

- Reference PPT missing or unreadable → log WARNING, set `match_filter_status: unavailable`,
  continue pipeline with zero exclusions. Never fail the review run.
- Partial parse (some slides unreadable) → match only the readable slides; log which were skipped.

### What the UI shows

- A **"Design Matched"** badge on slides that were excluded from design review.
- Tooltip: "This slide matches the approved reference template. Design agents were not applied."
- The Proposal Coverage Matrix and content findings still appear for matched slides.

### Implementation checklist

- [ ] `SlideDesignMatchService` in `services/slide_design_match.py`
- [ ] `reference_template_ppt` added as optional field to Review Request schema
- [ ] `matched_slide_indices` added to review context passed to all agents
- [ ] Template Agent and Branding Agent check `matched_slide_indices` before emitting findings
- [ ] `reference_template_id` + `reference_template_version` added to audit trail
- [ ] `slide_design_match` block added to Review Profile schema
- [ ] DB table `reference_templates` (additive migration, idempotent)
- [ ] Upload endpoint for reference PPT in `api/review.py`
- [ ] "Design Matched" badge in React results UI
- [ ] Unit tests: exact match, partial match, no match, missing reference PPT

---

## 13. Proposal Context Agent

### What it is

A dedicated **Proposal Context Agent** (`agent_id: proposal_context`) that evaluates whether the
presentation satisfies proposal requirements, client objectives, scope, and win themes.

This agent is distinct from all other agents:

| Agent            | Question it answers                                                              |
|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Customer/CIO Lens | "Is this appropriate and compelling for the executive audience?"                |
| Story Agent      | "Does this tell a coherent story?"                                               |
| Proposal Agent   | "Does this presentation satisfy and support the proposal requirements?"          |

### Proposal Context as a first-class input

```
Review Request
├── PPTX
├── Review Profile
├── Reference Template PPT  (optional)
└── Proposal Context        (optional)
      ├── Client
      ├── Opportunity
      ├── Objective
      ├── Requirements
      ├── Scope
      ├── Deliverables
      ├── Pain Points
      ├── Win Themes
      ├── Differentiators
      ├── Evaluation Criteria
      ├── Mandatory Content
      ├── Constraints
      └── Additional Guidance
```

Do not put proposal guidance into the system prompt. It is a versioned, structured input — not a
prompt string.

### Proposal Context schema

```yaml
proposal_context:
  client: "ABC Bank"

  opportunity:
    name: "Digital Banking Modernization"
    objective: "Modernize digital banking platform"

  audience:
    primary: "CIO"
    secondary: ["CTO", "Business Leadership"]

  business_problem:
    - "Legacy platform has scalability limitations"
    - "High operational cost"
    - "Slow feature delivery"

  requirements:
    - id: REQ-001
      requirement: "Cloud migration strategy"
      mandatory: true
    - id: REQ-002
      requirement: "API modernization"
      mandatory: true
    - id: REQ-003
      requirement: "Security architecture"
      mandatory: true

  deliverables:
    - "Target architecture"
    - "Migration roadmap"
    - "Implementation plan"

  win_themes:
    - "Accelerated modernization"
    - "Reduced operational cost"
    - "Low-risk migration"

  differentiators:
    - "Reusable modernization framework"
    - "Accelerator-based migration"

  evaluation_criteria:
    - "Technical capability"
    - "Delivery approach"
    - "Security"
    - "Cost optimization"

  constraints:
    - "Existing core banking system must remain operational"
    - "Migration must happen incrementally"
```

### DAG position

The Proposal Agent runs in parallel with Content, Story, and Customer agents after content extraction.
It does NOT depend on the CIO Lens. The Critic reconciles its findings with all other agents.

```
Content Extraction
    ├── Content Agent
    ├── Story Agent
    ├── Customer Agent
    └── Proposal Context Agent  ← parallel, independent
              │
              ▼
     Cross-Slide / CIO Lens
```

### Agent Registry entry

```yaml
agent_id: proposal_context
name: Proposal Context Agent
type: llm
version: 1
execution_mode: parallel
dependency_stage: content_extraction
requires:
  - ppt_content
  - proposal_context
severity_default: MEDIUM
enabled_by_default: false
```

### Review Profile integration

```json
{
  "review_profile": "customer_proposal",
  "agents": [
    { "id": "template",         "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "branding",         "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "customer_focus",   "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "storytelling",     "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "proposal_context", "enabled": true  },
    { "id": "cio_lens",         "enabled": true  }
  ]
}
```

For internal presentations: `{ "id": "proposal_context", "enabled": false }` — no code change required.

### Eight review dimensions

**A. Requirement Coverage** — Is every mandatory requirement addressed?

```
REQ-001 Cloud migration strategy → Slides 12–14 → COVERED
REQ-003 Security architecture   → No evidence  → MISSING
```

Coverage values: `COVERED | PARTIALLY_COVERED | MISSING | CONTRADICTED | NOT_APPLICABLE`

**B. Requirement-to-Slide Traceability** — Evidence chain per requirement:

```
REQ-002 API Modernization
  Slides: 15 (API Architecture), 16 (API Migration Approach)
  Evidence: API gateway, microservices, migration phases
  Coverage: STRONG  Confidence: 0.94
```

**C. Win Theme Coverage** — Are win themes demonstrated with evidence?

```
Win Theme: "Low-risk migration"
  ✓ Phased migration  ✓ Parallel run  ✓ Rollback strategy
  Coverage: STRONG

Win Theme: "Reduced operational cost"
  ✗ No quantified savings  ✗ No TCO comparison
  Coverage: WEAK
```

**D. Client Pain → Solution → Value chain** — Full value proposition present?

```
Pain → Solution → Capability → Business Outcome → Evidence
```

Flag if the deck only says "We will modernise your platform using AWS" with no outcome or evidence.

**E. Scope and Deliverable Alignment** — Missing or out-of-scope content?

```
Required: Migration + Testing + Training
Deck has: Migration, Testing
Missing:  Training  → HIGH finding
```

**F. Contradictions** — Conflicts between proposal context and slides?

```
Proposal Context: "Migration must be incremental"
Slide 21: "Complete migration during a single cutover"
→ CRITICAL finding, action_type: HUMAN_APPROVAL (never AUTO_FIX)
```

**G. Differentiation** — Are differentiators visible, explained, and evidenced?

```
Claim: "Accelerator-based migration"
Deck: Mentioned once, no evidence, no competitive comparison
→ RECOMMENDATION_ONLY finding
```

**H. Proposal Completeness** — Summary scorecard:

```
Requirements:  18 total / 15 covered / 2 partial / 1 missing
Win Themes:     3 total / 2 strong   / 1 weak
Deliverables:   6 total / 5 covered  / 1 missing
Contradictions: 1
Overall Score:  78
Readiness:      REVIEW REQUIRED
```

### Proposal Coverage Matrix

The agent internally produces a structured matrix before generating findings:

```
REQ-001 | Cloud migration       | Slides 12–14 | COVERED  | 95%
REQ-002 | API modernization     | Slides 15–16 | COVERED  | 92%
REQ-003 | Security architecture | Slide 18     | PARTIAL  | 81%
REQ-004 | Training              | —            | MISSING  | 99%
```

This matrix is returned as part of the agent output and drives a dedicated **Proposal Coverage** tab
in the React UI.

### Agent Contract output

```json
{
  "agent_id": "proposal_context",
  "status": "completed",
  "score": 82,
  "findings": [
    {
      "slide": 21,
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "category": "requirement_coverage",
      "title": "Security architecture requirement not sufficiently addressed",
      "description": "The proposal context requires a security architecture but the deck contains only high-level statements.",
      "recommendation": "Add a security architecture view covering identity, network security, data protection and monitoring.",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "action_type": "HUMAN_APPROVAL",
      "rule_id": "proposal.REQ-003",
      "prompt_version_id": 3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The proposal addresses most requirements but has gaps in security evidence and one weakly supported win theme."
}
```

### Action type discipline

| action_type          | Examples                                                                              |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `AUTO_FIX`           | Missing proposal label, formatting consistency, known structural metadata             |
| `HUMAN_APPROVAL`     | Missing requirement, contradiction, scope change, incorrect client claim, pricing, legal |
| `RECOMMENDATION_ONLY`| Weak differentiation, weak business value, weak evidence, generic messaging           |

**Hard rule:** Never `AUTO_FIX` business commitments, scope changes, pricing, or contractual matters.

### Prompt location

`prompts/proposal_context/v1.yaml` — versioned, immutable once created, never inlined in agent code.

The prompt must instruct the model to:
- Evaluate against requirements, not generic presentation quality.
- Classify every requirement as `COVERED / PARTIALLY_COVERED / MISSING / CONTRADICTED / NOT_APPLICABLE`.
- Never invent evidence not present in the PPT.
- Never assume an implied capability satisfies a mandatory requirement.
- Return only the structured Agent Contract.

### Versioning and auditability

Every review run audit trail includes:

```
PPT Version
+ Proposal Context Version
+ Review Profile Version
+ Proposal Prompt Version
+ Agent Version
```

This answers: "Why did Proposal Agent score this 74 last month but 86 today?"

### Implementation checklist

- [ ] Add `proposal_context` to Agent Registry
- [ ] Create `prompts/proposal_context/v1.yaml` with explicit output schema
