## 14. LLM Provider architecture — configurable, swappable, never hardcoded

### Design principle

No agent, service, or utility knows which LLM it is talking to. Every LLM call goes through the
`LLMProvider` ABC. The concrete provider is resolved once at startup by `ProviderFactory` reading
`LLM_PROVIDER` from `.env`. Swapping from the InfoBeans gateway to Groq is a one-line `.env` change
with zero code changes.

### Provider interface (`llm/provider.py`)

```python
class LLMProvider(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def complete(self, prompt: str, schema: dict | None = None, **kwargs) -> dict:
        """
        Send prompt, return parsed structured response.
        If schema is provided, enforce structured output at the provider level.
        Never return raw text — always return a dict.
        """

    @abstractmethod
    def health(self) -> bool:
        """Return True if the provider is reachable."""
```

`ProviderFactory.from_env()` reads `LLM_PROVIDER` and returns the correct concrete instance.
Agents call `ProviderFactory.from_env()` once (or receive it via dependency injection) — they never
instantiate a provider directly.

### Supported providers

| `LLM_PROVIDER` value | Class                  | Backing service                        |
|----------------------|------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| `gateway` (default)  | `GatewayProvider`      | InfoBeans gateway (`gateway.creatingwow.in`) |
| `litellm`            | `LiteLLMProvider`      | LiteLLM proxy — routes to any model    |
| `claude`             | `ClaudeProvider`       | Anthropic Claude API (direct)          |
| `groq`               | `GroqProvider`         | Groq API (direct)                      |
| `gemini`             | `GeminiProvider`       | Google Gemini API (direct)             |
| `openai`             | `OpenAIProvider`       | OpenAI API (direct)                    |
| `mock`               | `MockProvider`         | Deterministic test doubles — no network |

### `.env` reference

```dotenv
# ── LLM Provider selection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One of: gateway | litellm | claude | groq | gemini | openai | mock
LLM_PROVIDER=gateway

# ── InfoBeans Gateway (LLM_PROVIDER=gateway) ────────────────────────────────
GATEWAY_URL=https://gateway.creatingwow.in
GATEWAY_API_KEY=<your-key>
GATEWAY_MODEL=gpt-4o                        # model name forwarded to the gateway
GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# ── LiteLLM proxy (LLM_PROVIDER=litellm) ────────────────────────────────────
LITELLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000
LITELLM_API_KEY=<your-litellm-key>
LITELLM_MODEL=claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022    # any model string LiteLLM accepts

# ── Anthropic Claude direct (LLM_PROVIDER=claude) ───────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<your-anthropic-key>
CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022
CLAUDE_MAX_TOKENS=4096

# ── Groq direct (LLM_PROVIDER=groq) ─────────────────────────────────────────
GROQ_API_KEY=<your-groq-key>
GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile

# ── Google Gemini direct (LLM_PROVIDER=gemini) ──────────────────────────────
GEMINI_API_KEY=<your-gemini-key>
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-1.5-pro

# ── OpenAI direct (LLM_PROVIDER=openai) ─────────────────────────────────────
OPENAI_API_KEY=<your-openai-key>
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1   # override for Azure OpenAI or proxies

# ── Shared call settings (apply to all providers unless overridden) ──────────
LLM_TEMPERATURE=0.2
LLM_MAX_RETRIES=3
LLM_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS=2
```

Never commit a real key. `.env` is in `.gitignore`. Use `.env.example` with placeholder values for
documentation.

### How to add a new provider

1. Create `llm/providers/my_provider.py` implementing `LLMProvider`.
2. Add `"myprovider": MyProvider` to the `_REGISTRY` dict in `ProviderFactory`.
3. Add the relevant env vars to `.env.example` with comments.
4. Write a `MockProvider`-backed unit test — do not call the real API in tests.
5. Document the new `LLM_PROVIDER` value in the table above.

No other file changes required.

### Structured output contract

Every provider implementation must honour the `schema` parameter:

- If the underlying API supports native structured/JSON output (OpenAI `response_format`, Gemini
  `response_schema`, Anthropic tool-use), use it.
- If not (e.g. a plain-text gateway), append the JSON schema to the prompt and parse + validate the
  response before returning.
- If parsing fails after `LLM_MAX_RETRIES` attempts, raise `LLMStructuredOutputError` — never return
  a partially parsed or free-text dict.

Agents must never receive raw text from a provider call. The provider boundary is where text becomes
structure.

### Audit logging

Every provider call must write one row to `llm_request`:

```
provider        TEXT     -- e.g. "groq"
model           TEXT     -- exact model string used
prompt_hash     TEXT     -- SHA-256 of the rendered prompt
input_tokens    INT
output_tokens   INT
latency_ms      INT
status          TEXT     -- "success" | "error"
error_message   TEXT
agent_run_id    FK
prompt_version_id FK
```

This is how "why did this agent call cost 4 000 tokens last week but 1 200 today?" is answerable.

### Testing rule

`MockProvider` returns deterministic fixtures keyed on `prompt_hash`. Tests must never set
`LLM_PROVIDER` to anything other than `mock`. If a test file imports a real provider class directly,
that's a bug.

### Implementation checklist

- [ ] `llm/provider.py` — `LLMProvider` ABC + `ProviderFactory.from_env()`
- [ ] `llm/providers/gateway_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/litellm_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/claude_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/groq_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/gemini_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/openai_provider.py`
- [ ] `llm/providers/mock_provider.py`
- [ ] `.env.example` with all vars documented
- [ ] `LLMStructuredOutputError` raised on parse failure after retries
- [ ] `llm_request` audit table (additive migration)
- [ ] Unit test per provider using `MockProvider` fixtures
- [ ] Health-check endpoint (`/api/v2/health`) reports active provider + `health()` result

